module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.StrictHenselizationCanonicalMap
public import WeilConjectures.Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Sites.EtalePoint
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.Grp.Colimits
public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.IsSepClosed
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.SpecFiniteProductFields
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.StrictLocalBaseAffine
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.StrictLocalBaseIndEtale
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.ZeroDimensionalSpectrumProfinite

/-! # Global germs on a geometric strict-local base
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0EZV, corrected all-lifts proof, steps 1--3; Tag 03PN.
-/

@[expose] public section
universe u
set_option autoImplicit false
open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Etale

/-- The geometric stalks of the strict-local base are generated by global germs.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0EZV, finite-product presentation; Tags 09AY and 03PN.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-strict-local-base-presentation).

Proof: strictLocalBase_isAffine makes E affine, and strictLocalBase_indEtale
identifies its canonical K-algebra as ind-etale. Write it as a filtered colimit of
etale K-algebras. Every such algebra is a finite product of finite separable field
extensions, hence copies of K. Therefore all its primes are maximal, and all its local rings
are canonically K: at a prime, the idempotent of the selected factor kills the difference
between each represented element and its selected scalar; nonzero selected scalars
invert every denominator outside the prime. This also proves that its space is profinite.

At any geometric point of E, affine etale pointed neighbourhoods admit sections over a
smaller clopen neighbourhood. Indeed the selected fibre point is K-rational. Write the
images of finitely many algebra generators in the local ring as fractions; invert their
denominators and finitely many elements annihilating the relations. This extends the
pointed map to a principal neighbourhood, inside which one chooses a clopen.
Every germ has a representative on an affine pointed neighbourhood, so pull it back
to the clopen refinement. Extend this section by zero over its disjoint complement,
using the sheaf axiom. Naturality of pointed germ maps shows that the resulting global
section has the original germ. Equalization of refinements is unnecessary for this
surjectivity assertion.

The empty scheme has no points and the assertion on germs is vacuous. No torsion or
finiteness hypothesis on a sheaf is used. -/
theorem strictLocalBase_globalGerms
    {S : Scheme.{u}} {Ω : Type u} [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω]
    (s : Spec (.of Ω) ⟶ S) (K : Type u) [Field K] [IsSepClosed K]
    (u : Spec (.of K) ⟶ Scheme.strictHenselizationScheme s) :
    let E := pullback (Scheme.strictHenselizationStructuralMap s)
      (u ≫ Scheme.strictHenselizationStructuralMap s)
    ∀ (F : Sheaf E.smallEtaleTopology AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (e : E),
        Function.Surjective
          ((Scheme.pointSmallEtale (E.algClosurePoint e)).toPresheafFiber
            (Scheme.Etale.mk (𝟙 E))
            (Over.homMk (E.algClosurePoint e) (Category.comp_id _)) F.obj) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Etale
